<header>
  <h1>Gerotranscendent Generativity:  How Older Adults Maintain a Focus on &ldquo;Being&rdquo; for Others</h1>
  <div class="authors">Theresa Southam, MA, PhD Student; Fielding Graduate University</div>
</header>

<div class="poster-body">

  <!-- Column 1: Abstract, Research Goals -->
  <div class="column" style="flex: 1.15;">
    <h2 class="section-title">Abstract</h2>
    <p>Generativity in older adults has predominantly been defined as tangible assets delivered to others, including caring for others, civic action, and community volunteerism (Hodgkin, 2012; De St Aubin &amp; McAdams, 2004). Less light has been shone on non-physical contributions. In this research we learn how generative older adults focus on being rather than doing, while becoming what Edmondson (2015) calls socio-cultural message bearers, advisors on the human condition and creators of cultural and artistic products and practices.</p>

    <h2 class="section-title">Research Goals, Participants and Methods</h2>
    <p>This ethnographic and phenomenologically informed research asked <span class="italic">&ldquo;What does it look like when older adults&rsquo; are contributing to the next generations from a gerotranscendent lens?&rdquo;</span></p>
    <p>Over two months, the researcher observed:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>14 participants in Intergenerational Leadership and Learning class co-facilitated by researcher.</li>
      <li>Eight older adults recommended by a hospice society interlocutor who participated in two, 3-hour sessions: autobiographical life review (Wengraf, 2004) and life-cross roads (Keisari &amp; Palgi, 2017).</li>
      <li>Three participants randomly selected from whole sample for phenomenological interviews.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="fractal-placeholder placeholder" style="height:190px;">[Fractal/circle-packing diagram: bottom-left]</div>
  </div>

  <!-- Column 2: Results, Typifications, Overarching Theme, Autobiographical -->
  <div class="column" style="flex: 1.25;">
    <h2 class="section-title">Results</h2>
    <p>Records of group discussions, artwork, life crossroads diagrams, a survey and observations were coded as typifications &ndash; groupings based on shared experiences. A theme, raised independently by the participants during the group discussions and vigourously agreed on by all, is highlighted.</p>

    <h3 class="sub-title">Typifications</h3>
    <p><strong>Elders/Sages/Mentors:</strong> focus on just being by talking to people that no one will talk to, acting in a way that teaches and shows, communing with nature, &ldquo;adopting&rdquo; children that need soul grandparents, peer counseling and bringing spirit into the body.</p>
    <p><strong>Third Agers:</strong> focus on doing by being remembered for actions such as reinvigorating a community organization, advocating for more environmentally sound transportation, volunteering, and tutoring.</p>

    <h3 class="sub-title">Overarching Theme</h3>
    <p>A focus on <span class="italic">being</span> rather than <span class="italic">doing</span></p>
    <p class="quote">&ldquo;I feel sorry for people trying to save the world, I have a quieter more contemplative life at 70 then I had at 55.&rdquo;</p>
    <p class="quote">&ldquo;The older I get, people look to me, I have so much gratitude.&rdquo;</p>

    <h3 class="sub-title">Autobiographical and Life&rsquo;s Crossroads: Diagramming Life&rsquo;s Coherence and Meaning</h3>
    <p>To the right, artwork and phrases that best represent the collective responses are provided. The survey is summarized in the chart.</p>

