        <div class="fig-caption">Figure 3: Participant Drawing of Cultivating Gratitude and Heart.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="fig-text"><span class="label">Rituals &amp; Practices-</span> Contemplation, time in nature, meditation, yoga, and tai chi practices that cultivate gratitude, heart wisdom, grounding and forgiveness.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="fig-block">
      <div>
        <div class="fig-img placeholder">[Drawing: figures holding light]</div>
        <div class="fig-caption">Figure 4: Holding Someone in the Light.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="fig-text"><span class="label">Life Principles-</span> believing all are equal, in goodness, the law of impermanence, and a connecting spirit.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="fig-block">
      <div>
        <div class="fig-img placeholder">[Sketch: community, figures and trees]</div>
        <div class="fig-caption">Figure 5: Community Support</div>
      </div>
      <div class="fig-text"><span class="label">Community Supports-</span> Nature, connection &ldquo;being seen.&rdquo;</div>
    </div>

    <div class="acknowledgements">
      <strong>Acknowledgements</strong><br>
      This research was partly funded by a Social Transformation Grant from the Building Inclusive Collaborative of the Marie Fielder Center for Democracy, Leadership and Education at Fielding Graduate University
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Column 4: Conclusions and Next Steps -->
  <div class="column" style="flex: 1.1;">
    <h2 class="section-title">Conclusions and Next Steps</h2>
    <p>Gerotranscendence described as where the boundaries between self and the universe dissolve.</p>
    <p>Study participants agree on a shift in emphasis from age 55 where they were doing to 70 when they are being.</p>
    <p class="quote-red">&ldquo;People measure how much we give materialistically. They know there&rsquo;s more and they feel compelled to share love.&rdquo; Quote from research participant.</p>

    <h3 class="sub-title">Next Steps</h3>
    <p>Although older adults are largely defined in contemporary EuroAmerican culture as a burden, they are in fact abundantly generative. The ability of older adults to continue learning, leading spiritual, artistic and contemplative lives, is supported by neurological studies (Cohen, 2005). Further research on the impact on the collective consciousness of older adults&rsquo;messages, advice and creations is warranted.</p>

    <div class="fractal-placeholder placeholder">[Fractal/circle-packing diagram: center]</div>
